You are working in /opt/nxeon-v2 (branch design/v2), a design fork of the Nxeon hosting platform. READ /opt/nxeon-v2/CLAUDE.md FIRST for non-negotiable rules (most important: never fabricate; never render a UI for something the backend can't do).

CRITICAL CONCURRENCY RULES:
- Another agent (me) is working in this same tree RIGHT NOW. Stay strictly inside the files listed below. Do NOT touch anything under components/customer/hosting/ (except NOT AT ALL), components/customer/vpspanel/, app/dashboard/hosting/, prisma/schema.prisma, or lib/actions-hosting.ts.
- NEVER manually touch, rename, delete or "clean up" `app/(marketing)` or `app/_marketing_skipped`. If you see `app/_marketing_skipped` it means another process is mid-build — leave it alone.
- Do NOT run ./rebuild.sh. Do NOT run git commit/add/reset. Do NOT run npm run db:push.
- You MAY run `cd /opt/nxeon-v2 && npx tsc --noEmit` to typecheck (note it will also report errors from MY in-progress files under components/customer/hosting/v2/ and lib/actions-hosting-*.ts — ignore those, they're mine; only fix errors in YOUR files).

YOUR TASK: refactor the shared file manager so it can be driven by a pluggable data source, then add a hosting data source. Today it is hardwired to a VPS `serverId`.

Files you own for this task:
- components/customer/files/FileBrowser.tsx  (refactor)
- components/customer/files/UploadQueue.tsx  (refactor)
- lib/files-types.ts  (add the adapter type)
- lib/files-adapters.ts  (NEW — client-side adapter factories)
- lib/actions-hosting-fs.ts (NEW — "use server" file, the hosting-side file actions)
- app/api/hosting/files/route.ts (extend — see below)
- app/dashboard/servers/[id]/page.tsx (update the two FileBrowser call sites only)

=== STEP 1: the adapter type (lib/files-types.ts) ===
Add exactly this type (keep the existing FileEntry unchanged; note the existing file-header comment explains why types live here and not in a "use server" file — respect that):

export type FileMut = { ok: true } | { ok: false; error: string };
export type FileApi = {
  /** Stable identity for this source — used as a React remount key. */
  key: string;
  /** Label shown as the root breadcrumb, e.g. "root" or "public_html". */
  rootLabel: string;
  list(path: string): Promise<{ ok: true; entries: FileEntry[] } | { ok: false; error: string }>;
  read(path: string): Promise<{ ok: true; content: string } | { ok: false; error: string }>;
  save(path: string, content: string): Promise<FileMut>;
  mkdir(dir: string, name: string): Promise<FileMut>;
  rename(path: string, newName: string): Promise<FileMut>;
  remove(path: string, isDirectory: boolean): Promise<FileMut>;
  /** URL accepting a multipart POST with field `file`; used by the XHR upload queue. */
  uploadUrl(dir: string): string;
  /** A direct download URL for one file, or null when the source has no download route. */
  downloadUrl?(path: string): string | null;
  /** Optional chmod; omit when the source cannot change modes. */
  chmod?(path: string, mode: string): Promise<FileMut>;
};

Inside FileBrowser, paths are the browser's own "/"-rooted virtual paths exactly as today. Each adapter is responsible for translating them to whatever its backend wants.

=== STEP 2: lib/files-adapters.ts (NEW, "use client"-safe plain module, no "use server") ===
Export two factories:

`export function serverFileApi(serverId: string): FileApi` — wraps the EXISTING actions in lib/actions-files.ts (listFiles, readFile, saveFile, createFolder, renameEntry, deleteEntry, chmodEntry) and uses uploadUrl `/api/files/${serverId}?path=${encodeURIComponent(dir)}`. key = `server:${serverId}`, rootLabel = "root". Behaviour must be byte-for-byte what the VPS file browser does today — read lib/actions-files.ts carefully and match every signature and result shape (note some return `{ok:true}|{ok:false,error}` already; adapt where shapes differ). Preserve the existing `deleteEntry(serverId, path)` signature (it takes no isDirectory — just ignore the isDirectory arg in this adapter).

`export function hostingFileApi(accountId: string, root: string): FileApi` — `root` is a chroot-relative base like "public_html" or "domains/example.com/public_html" or "" (whole account home). The browser's "/" maps to `root`; "/a/b" maps to `${root}/a/b` (strip a leading "/" and join with "/", collapsing empty segments — if root is "" then "/" maps to "" i.e. the account home). key = `hosting:${accountId}:${root}`, rootLabel = the last path segment of root, or "home" when root is "". uploadUrl = `/api/hosting/files?accountId=${accountId}&path=${encodeURIComponent(<mapped dir>)}`. downloadUrl = `/api/hosting/files?accountId=${accountId}&path=${encodeURIComponent(<mapped path>)}`. No chmod (omit the property) — the hosting backend has no chmod, and per the tree's rules we must not render a control that cannot work.

=== STEP 3: lib/actions-hosting-fs.ts (NEW "use server" file) ===
The hosting file actions that exist today live in lib/actions-hosting.ts — `hostingListFiles`, `hostingMkdir`, `hostingDeleteFile` (around line 1425). DO NOT EDIT lib/actions-hosting.ts (I'm editing it). Instead create lib/actions-hosting-fs.ts exporting a COMPLETE set that the adapter uses:

  hostingFsList(accountId, relPath) -> { ok: true, entries: FileEntry[] } | { ok: false, error }
  hostingFsRead(accountId, relPath) -> { ok: true, content: string } | { ok: false, error }
  hostingFsSave(accountId, relPath, content) -> FileMut
  hostingFsMkdir(accountId, dirRelPath, name) -> FileMut
  hostingFsRename(accountId, relPath, newName) -> FileMut
  hostingFsDelete(accountId, relPath, isDirectory) -> FileMut

Implementation notes — read these files first: lib/hosting-fs.ts (fsTargetFor, fsList, fsMkdir, fsDelete, fsReadBuffer, fsWriteBuffer, fsStatSize, safeRel) and the existing three actions in lib/actions-hosting.ts so you copy their ownership/permission discipline EXACTLY:
- Each action must do the same owner-or-admin + status===ACTIVE check the existing ones do (they use a local helper `ownedAccountWithNode`; you will need to write an equivalent in your new file — replicate its exact semantics: requireUser(), find the account including node, return an error string for not-found / not-owner-and-not-admin / not-ACTIVE).
- Honour `SANDBOX` from lib/sandbox exactly like the existing actions do: under SANDBOX, mutations return ok without touching anything, and `hostingFsList` returns the same kind of stub the existing `hostingListFiles` returns. IMPORTANT: make the SANDBOX list stub a little more useful than today's single hardcoded "public_html" entry, but DO NOT invent fake customer data — return a SMALL, obviously-structural stub and make sure any UI-visible text calls it what it is. Simplest honest choice: under SANDBOX return an EMPTY entry list, since there is no real filesystem to read; the browser will render its normal "this folder is empty" state. Prefer that over inventing files. Same for read: return `{ ok: false, error: "No hosting node is attached in this environment, so there are no files to read." }`.
- `hostingFsRead`: cap at 1 MB (use fsStatSize first and refuse above the cap with a clear error), decode as utf8.
- `hostingFsSave`: write via fsWriteBuffer.
- `hostingFsRename`: lib/hosting-fs.ts has NO rename primitive. Add one — `fsRename(t, fromRel, toRel)` in lib/hosting-fs.ts using the ssh2 SFTPWrapper's `rename` (follow the exact `withSftp` + `safeRel` pattern the other primitives use; you may edit lib/hosting-fs.ts, it is yours for this task). Rename must stay in the SAME parent directory (derive the new path from the old path's parent + sanitized new name) — matching how the VPS `renameEntry` behaves. Sanitize the new name to /^[A-Za-z0-9._-]{1,120}$/-ish like hostingMkdir does.
- Convert lib/hosting-fs.ts's `FsEntry {name,isDir,size,modified}` into the shared `FileEntry {name,isDirectory,size,modifiedMs,mode}` shape. `mode` should be the REAL octal mode if SFTP gives you one — check what fsList currently reads from the SFTP attrs; if the mode is genuinely available, plumb it through (edit fsList to return it); if it is not available, return `null`, never a made-up "644".

=== STEP 4: app/api/hosting/files/route.ts ===
It already has POST (upload) and GET (download). Check whether its `path` handling works for a nested path under a website docroot (it currently does `${dir}/${safeName}` for uploads) — make sure an arbitrary nested `path` query param works and is still confined (lib/hosting-fs.ts's `safeRel` is the confinement; verify it's applied on both paths and add it if it is not). Do not weaken any existing limit (MAX_UPLOAD / MAX_DOWNLOAD) or auth check.

=== STEP 5: refactor the components ===
- `useUploadQueue(serverId, path, onDone)` becomes `useUploadQueue(api: FileApi, path: string, onDone: () => void)` and builds the XHR URL from `api.uploadUrl(path)` instead of the hardcoded `/api/files/...`. Everything else about it (XHR progress, one-at-a-time, beginUpload/endUpload) stays EXACTLY as is — do not regress the real byte-progress behaviour.
- `FileBrowser({ serverId })` becomes `FileBrowser({ api, initialPath })` where initialPath is an optional "/"-rooted path to open at (default "/"). Replace every direct `listFiles/readFile/saveFile/createFolder/renameEntry/deleteEntry/chmodEntry` call with the corresponding `api.*` call. `TreeNode` takes the api too.
- Where the UI currently offers chmod, render that control ONLY when `api.chmod` is defined. Same for download if the browser currently offers one.
- Read the whole of FileBrowser.tsx before editing. Keep every existing feature: folder tree with lazy nodes, breadcrumbs, search, sorting, multi-select, bulk delete, inline editor, upload drop zone, upload queue. Keep the "DELIBERATELY ABSENT: move and copy" comment and behaviour. Keep all existing styling and theme tokens exactly (this tree has a light theme; do not introduce hardcoded colors — use the existing token classes already in the file).
- Update the file's top doc comment to explain the adapter indirection and WHY (one product, two backends: a VPS over SSH and a shared-hosting account over SFTP), in the same voice as the existing comment.

=== STEP 6: update the VPS call sites ===
app/dashboard/servers/[id]/page.tsx lines ~590 and ~605 render `<FileBrowser serverId={server.id} />`. Change to `<FileBrowser api={serverFileApi(server.id)} />`. NOTE: that page is a server component — check whether calling a client factory there works. If `serverFileApi` cannot be called from a server component (it returns closures over server actions, which are not serializable across the RSC boundary), then instead give FileBrowser an optional convenience: accept `api: FileApi` OR keep a thin client wrapper component (e.g. `ServerFileBrowser({ serverId })` exported from components/customer/files/FileBrowser.tsx, marked "use client", that builds the adapter internally and renders FileBrowser). Choose whichever actually compiles and keeps the RSC boundary clean, and say which you chose in your report. Do not change anything else on that page.

=== DONE CRITERIA ===
- `cd /opt/nxeon-v2 && npx tsc --noEmit` reports NO errors in any file you touched (ignore errors under components/customer/hosting/v2/, lib/actions-hosting-sites.ts, lib/hosting-thumb.ts, lib/actions-hosting.ts and prisma-client type errors about new hosting models — those are mine, in flight).
- No behaviour regression for the VPS file browser.

Report back: what you changed file by file, the exact final signature of `FileApi`, `serverFileApi`, `hostingFileApi`, `FileBrowser` and `useUploadQueue` (I am building UI against them, so I need them exact), which approach you took for the server-component call site, and anything you found that the hosting backend genuinely cannot do (so I can keep the UI honest).

